NAME
       getent - look up entries in the system databases (users, groups, hosts)

SYNOPSIS
       getent DATABASE [KEY...]

DESCRIPTION
       getent looks things up the same way programs do, through the name
       service switch (/etc/nsswitch.conf). That means it finds local
       entries from files such as /etc/passwd and /etc/hosts and also those
       from network sources (DNS, LDAP, IdM) when the system uses them.

       Without a KEY it prints the whole database (for the network ones, as
       far as they allow); with keys it prints only the matching entries,
       in the same format as the file. The exit status is 2 when a key is not
       found, which makes getent useful in scripts and checks.

DATABASES
       passwd     users (name or UID as key)
       group      groups (name or GID)
       shadow     password ageing data (root only)
       gshadow    group passwords and administrators (root only)
       hosts      host names and addresses, in the order nsswitch.conf
                  gives (usually /etc/hosts first, then DNS)
       ahosts     like hosts, listing every address
       services   network service names and ports, from /etc/services

EXAMPLES
       Does the user exist, and what are its UID and home?

           getent passwd student

       Which users are in the group devops?

           getent group devops

       How does this machine resolve a name (hosts file or DNS)?

           getent hosts server2.lab.example.com

       Which port is https?

           getent services https

SEE ALSO
       id(1), nsswitch.conf(5), passwd(5), group(5), hosts(5)
